WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.840
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.400
This work is about improving reinforcement
learning.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.549
Reinforcement learning is a learning algorithm
that we can use to choose a set of actions

00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:15.150
in an environment to maximize a score.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:20.060
Our classical example applications are helicopter
control, where the score to be maximized would

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.449
be proportional to the distance that we traveled
safely, or any computer game of your choice

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:28.640
where a score can describe how well we are
doing.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.899
For instance, in Frostbite, our score describes
how many jumps we have survived without dying

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:36.640
and this score is subject to maximization.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.100
Earlier, scientists at DeepMind combined a
reinforcement learner with a deep neural network

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.809
so the algorithm could look at the screen
and play the game much like a human player

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:47.809
would.

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:51.170
This problem is especially difficult when
the rewards are sparse.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:55.510
This is similar to what a confused student
would experience after a written exam when

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.150
only one grade is given, but the results for
the individual problems are not shown.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:05.020
It is quite hard to know where we did well
and where we missed the mark, and it is much

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:09.730
more challenging to choose the appropriate
topics to study to do better next time.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:15.800
When starting out, the learner starts exploring
the parameter space and performs crazy, seemingly

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.940
nonsensical actions until it finds a few scenarios
where it is able to do well.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.740
This can be thought of as adding noise to
the actions of the agent.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.790
Scientists at OpenAI proposed an approach
where they add noise not directly to the actions,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:36.380
but the parameters of the agent, which results
in perturbations that depend on the information

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.110
that the agent senses.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:43.330
This leads to less flailing and a more systematic
exploration that substantially decreases the

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.310
time taken to learn tasks with sparse rewards.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:50.960
For instance, it makes a profound difference
if we use it in the walker game.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.240
As you can see here, the algorithm with the
parameter space noise is able to learn the

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.460
concept of galloping, while the traditional
method does, well, I am not sure what it is

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.040
doing to be honest, but it is significantly
less efficient.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.350
The solution does not come without challenges.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:11.849
For instance, different layers respond differently
to this added noise, and, the effect of the

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:16.599
noise on the outputs grows over time, which
requires changing the amount of noise to be

00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:20.250
added depending on its expected effect on
the output.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.209
This technique is called adaptive noise scaling.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:27.500
There are plenty of comparisons and other
cool details in the paper, make sure to have

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.870
a look, it is available in the video description.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:36.249
DeepMind's deep reinforcement learning was
published in 2015 with some breathtaking results

00:02:36.249 --> 00:02:41.439
and superhuman plays on a number of different
games, and it has already been improved leaps

00:02:41.439 --> 00:02:44.340
and bounds beyond its initial version.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.290
And we are talking about OpenAI, so of course,
the source code of this project is available

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.810
under the permissive MIT license.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.510
In the meantime, we have recently been able
to upgrade the entirety of our sound recording

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.879
pipeline through your support on Patreon.

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:04.389
I have been yearning for this for a long-long
time now and not only that, but we could also

00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:10.569
extend our software pipeline with sound processing
units that use AI and work like magic.

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:12.590
Quite fitting for the series, right?

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:17.609
Next up is a recording room or recording corner
